          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>home generator for a power outage</strong> isn't just about comfort — for many households it's about safety, food and medication preservation, and preventing secondary damage like a flooded basement. The right choice depends heavily on your specific risk profile: how often and how long outages happen in your area, whether anyone in your household depends on powered medical equipment, and how much you're willing to spend for automatic, whole-home coverage versus a more affordable, manually operated setup.
