          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Types of Home Generators: Standby vs Portable vs Inverter vs Battery
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>best home generator for a power outage</strong> depends entirely on your budget, how much coverage you need, and how hands-on you're willing to be during an actual emergency. Here's how the four main categories compare:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Standby Generators</h3>
          <p>
            Permanently installed outside your home and wired into your electrical panel, standby generators start automatically within seconds of an outage, with no manual intervention required. They deliver true whole-home coverage, typically running on natural gas or propane for extended or indefinite runtime. This is the <strong>best home emergency generator for automatic, whole-house protection</strong>, but it requires professional installation and the highest upfront cost of any category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Portable Generators</h3>
          <p>
            Gas or dual-fuel engines on wheels, portable generators require manual setup, fueling, and either extension cords or a transfer switch to distribute power. They offer the best balance of output and cost for households wanting to cover most essentials without a permanent installation, and remain the most popular <strong>generator for power outages</strong> among homeowners.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Inverter Generators</h3>
          <p>
            A refined subset of portable generators, inverter models produce cleaner, more stable power safe for sensitive electronics, and run noticeably quieter than standard portables. They typically offer less raw wattage than large conventional portables, making them best suited to covering essentials rather than whole-home needs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery/Solar Generators</h3>
          <p>
            Using stored battery power rather than combustion, these units produce zero emissions and can safely run indoors, with instant, silent power delivery. Runtime is finite unless paired with solar panels for ongoing recharge, and cost per watt-hour is higher than fuel-powered options, but the safety and silence are unmatched.
          </p>

