          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Size Generator Do I Need for My House?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choosing the right wattage is the single most important decision in this category — undersized and you'll be constantly juggling which appliances can run at once; oversized and you're paying for capacity you'll never use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: List Your Must-Run Appliances</h3>
          <p>
            Start with genuine essentials: refrigerator, freezer, sump pump, well pump (if applicable), a handful of lights, and phone/device charging. Add anything medically necessary, like a CPAP machine or refrigerated medication storage, to this non-negotiable list first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Add Up Running Watts, Then Account for Starting Watts</h3>
          <p>
            Most appliances have both a running wattage and a higher starting (surge) wattage needed briefly when they first switch on — this matters most for anything with a motor, like a refrigerator, well pump, or air conditioner. Add up the running watts of everything you want to run simultaneously, then check that your generator's starting watt capacity can handle your single largest appliance's startup surge on top of everything else already running.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Decide on Central AC</h3>
          <p>
            Central air conditioning is the single biggest wattage consumer in most homes and the deciding factor in which category you need. Covering central AC realistically requires either a standby generator or one of the largest portable units, like the DuroMax XP12000EH — smaller inverter and portable generators simply don't have the headroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Build In a Buffer</h3>
          <p>
            Whatever total you calculate, add roughly 20% headroom rather than buying a generator sized to your exact current need. This accounts for appliance inefficiency, future additions to your essential list, and simply gives the generator an easier, less strained workload, which extends its usable lifespan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Safety -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Generator Safety During a Power Outage
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Single Most Important Safety Rule</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Never run a fuel-powered generator indoors, in a garage (even with the door open), or near windows, doors, or vents. Carbon monoxide from generator exhaust is odorless, colorless, and can be fatal within minutes at high concentration. Position any fuel-powered generator at least 20 feet from your home, with the exhaust pointed away from windows and doors, and install battery-powered carbon monoxide detectors inside your home as an additional safety layer.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Beyond carbon monoxide, a few other safety practices matter during real generator use. Never refuel a generator while it's running or still hot — spilled fuel on a hot engine is a genuine fire risk, and this is a common cause of generator-related injuries. Keep the generator dry and off the ground during wet weather using a generator canopy or covered, ventilated enclosure rather than running it exposed in the rain. And never connect a portable generator directly to your home's wiring without a properly installed transfer switch — doing so risks backfeeding electricity into utility lines, which can seriously injure or kill utility workers repairing the outage, in addition to being illegal in most jurisdictions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Transfer Switches -->
        <div id="transfer-switches" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Generator Transfer Switches Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            A transfer switch is the safe, legal way to connect a portable generator to your home's electrical panel, and it's the single upgrade that turns a portable generator from "power a few things via extension cord" into genuine whole-panel or whole-circuit backup power.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>manual transfer switch</strong>, installed by a licensed electrician, sits between your generator and your main panel. During an outage, you manually switch selected circuits from utility power to generator power, then start your generator and plug it into a dedicated inlet — no extension cords through windows required. This is the standard, cost-effective solution for large portable generators, typically adding $500-1,500 to installation cost depending on how many circuits you want covered.
          </p>

          <p>
            An <strong>automatic transfer switch</strong>, standard with standby generator installations, detects the outage and switches over automatically without any homeowner action, which is why standby systems can restore power even when nobody is home. Automatic switches are integrated into the standby generator installation cost rather than purchased separately.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Maintenance & Storage -->
        <div id="maintenance-storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Generator Maintenance and Fuel Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            A generator that hasn't been maintained is a generator that's likely to fail exactly when you need it most — during a storm, after months of sitting idle. Building a simple maintenance routine is one of the highest-value things you can do after buying any generator on this list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Portable and Inverter Generators</h3>
          <p>
            Run the generator under load for 20-30 minutes every 2-3 months, even when there's no outage, to keep the engine, carburetor, and battery in working condition. Change the oil per the manufacturer's schedule, typically every 50-100 hours of use, and inspect the air filter and spark plug at least once a season. If you store gasoline for the generator, use a fuel stabilizer and rotate the fuel supply every 6-12 months, since gasoline degrades and can gum up a carburetor if left sitting too long. Propane, by contrast, stores indefinitely without degrading, which is part of why dual-fuel units are appealing for emergency preparedness specifically.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Standby Generators</h3>
          <p>
            Standby units are largely self-maintaining thanks to their automatic weekly self-test cycle, which runs the engine briefly to verify it starts and charges its battery correctly. Even so, an annual professional service visit — checking oil, filters, spark plugs, and the battery — is worth scheduling, similar to servicing a furnace or air conditioner, since these systems run on the same combustion principles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Generators</h3>
          <p>
            Battery units need the least ongoing maintenance of any category — no oil, filters, or spark plugs — but lithium battery packs still benefit from being kept at a moderate charge level (roughly 40-60%) during long storage periods rather than left fully depleted or fully charged for months at a time, which extends long-term battery health.
          </p>
        </div>

