              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need to power my house during an outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For whole-home coverage including central AC, plan on 20-22kW for a standby generator. For a portable generator covering essentials like a refrigerator, well pump, lights, and a window AC unit, 5,000-9,000 running watts is typically sufficient. For just critical essentials — refrigerator, some lights, device charging — 2,000-2,500 watts is enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a portable or standby generator better for home power outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standby generators offer automatic, whole-home coverage that starts within seconds, even when you're not home, but require professional installation and a significantly higher upfront cost. Portable generators cost far less and offer more flexibility, but require manual setup, refueling, and either a transfer switch or extension cords to distribute power to specific circuits or appliances.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a generator inside my house or garage during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, never run a fuel-powered generator indoors, in a garage, or near windows and doors — carbon monoxide from the exhaust is odorless, fast-acting, and fatal, and this is one of the most common causes of generator-related deaths during outages. Fuel-powered generators must run outdoors, at least 20 feet from the home, with exhaust directed away from windows. Battery-based generators, like the EcoFlow Delta Pro, produce no exhaust and are safe to run indoors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a transfer switch for a portable generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A transfer switch is strongly recommended for any portable generator you intend to use for more than a couple of individual appliances. It safely connects the generator to your home's electrical panel, preventing backfeed into utility lines (a serious safety hazard to utility workers) and letting you power hardwired circuits like well pumps and central air without running extension cords through windows.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can a home generator run continuously during an outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standby generators running on natural gas can run effectively indefinitely as long as gas service continues. Propane-fueled standby or portable generators are limited by tank size. Gasoline-powered portable generators typically run 8-12 hours per tank at moderate load and require manual refueling, which should only be done after the engine has cooled to avoid fire risk.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes When Buying a Home Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generators are a genuinely important safety purchase, and getting the sizing or setup wrong undermines the entire point. These are the most frequent mistakes we see.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Based on Price Alone, Without Sizing First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The single most common mistake is buying whatever generator fits the budget without first adding up actual essential-appliance wattage. This leads to either an undersized unit that can't run what you actually need during an emergency, or unnecessary spending on capacity a household will never use. Do the wattage math before shopping, not after.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Transfer Switch to Save Money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running extension cords through windows to individual appliances is inefficient, can't power hardwired circuits like well pumps, and creates real safety hazards with cords crossing wet ground. A properly installed transfer switch is a worthwhile investment that makes any large portable generator dramatically more useful and safer during an actual emergency.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Testing the Generator Before an Emergency</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A generator that's sat unused in a garage for a year may not start reliably when you actually need it — stale fuel, a dead battery, or a mechanical issue are all common problems that surface at the worst possible time. Run and test any generator every few months, and use fuel stabilizer for gasoline-powered units stored for extended periods.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating Carbon Monoxide Risk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Placing a generator too close to the home, in a garage with the door cracked open, or under a covered porch are all still genuinely dangerous positions despite feeling like reasonable weather protection. Follow the 20-foot minimum distance guideline strictly, and install carbon monoxide detectors in your home regardless of where the generator is positioned.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
